WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:06.615
Rachel Kim: Alright, it’s 10:02, let’s get started. Hope everyone’s Friday is going okay?

2
00:00:07.062 --> 00:00:13.216
Diego Santos: Yeah, all good here. Just, uh, trying to keep my inbox under control before the weekend.

3
00:00:13.474 --> 00:00:19.628
Amara Nwosu: Same, Diego. I’m still catching up from that Salesforce sync issue on Wednesday, but otherwise good.

4
00:00:20.395 --> 00:00:25.395
Omar Haddad: Morning, all. I’m running on my third coffee, so I’m ready for anything.

5
00:00:26.271 --> 00:00:32.424
Nadia Petrova: Hi everyone! I’m just glad the Q3 pipeline review is behind us. That was a marathon.

6
00:00:33.673 --> 00:00:40.981
Rachel Kim: Yeah, Nadia, you did a great job wrangling all those numbers. Okay, quick status—anything urgent before we jump in?

7
00:00:42.074 --> 00:00:45.535
Diego Santos: Nothing urgent from me. Marketo’s behaving, knock on wood.

8
00:00:46.957 --> 00:00:51.957
Amara Nwosu: No blockers here. Just prepping for the next round of lead scoring QA.

9
00:00:53.116 --> 00:01:04.654
Rachel Kim: Alright, let’s move to the main agenda. So, we need to finalize the update to the Marketo trial threshold scoring. Everyone had a chance to look at the doc, right?

10
00:01:05.110 --> 00:01:12.418
Omar Haddad: Yep, I read through it. The main change is bumping the MQL threshold from 25 to 31 points, correct?

11
00:01:13.179 --> 00:01:19.332
Diego Santos: That’s right. Everything else in the scoring table stays the same, just the threshold goes up.

12
00:01:20.766 --> 00:01:28.074
Nadia Petrova: Can someone remind me why we’re increasing it? I remember the discussion but want to make sure I’m clear.

13
00:01:29.471 --> 00:01:40.240
Rachel Kim: Sure, Nadia. Basically, we saw too many trial users MQLing too early, especially with the new Duo features. Raising the threshold should help filter for more qualified leads.

14
00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:49.327
Amara Nwosu: Yeah, and the data from last month showed a spike in MQLs that didn’t convert. So, this should help with quality.

15
00:01:50.389 --> 00:01:55.389
Omar Haddad: I just want to flag—will this impact our MQL volume targets for Q3?

16
00:01:55.751 --> 00:02:04.982
Rachel Kim: Good question. Omar, I think we’ll see a dip at first, but the expectation is higher conversion rates. We’ll monitor and adjust if needed.

17
00:02:06.352 --> 00:02:12.121
Diego Santos: I can pull a report after two weeks to see how the numbers look post-change.

18
00:02:12.981 --> 00:02:19.135
Nadia Petrova: Sounds good to me. As long as Sales is looped in, I don’t see any issues.

19
00:02:20.201 --> 00:02:28.278
Amara Nwosu: I do have one concern—do we need to update any of the nurture streams, or is this just the scoring logic?

20
00:02:28.902 --> 00:02:36.595
Diego Santos: Just the scoring logic for now. Nurture streams trigger off MQL status, so they’ll pick up the new threshold automatically.

21
00:02:37.701 --> 00:02:40.778
Rachel Kim: Thanks, Diego. Amara, does that answer your question?

22
00:02:41.699 --> 00:02:43.237
Amara Nwosu: Yep, that’s clear. Thanks.

23
00:02:43.687 --> 00:02:48.302
Omar Haddad: And the scoring runs still twice a day, right? No change there.

24
00:02:49.366 --> 00:02:53.213
Diego Santos: Correct, still 8am and 2pm in each region’s earliest timezone.

25
00:02:53.905 --> 00:03:03.136
Rachel Kim: Alright, so to recap: we’re updating the MQL threshold for trial users from 25 to 31 points. Everything else stays the same. Any objections?

26
00:03:04.309 --> 00:03:05.847
Nadia Petrova: Nope, all good here.

27
00:03:06.273 --> 00:03:07.773
Omar Haddad: No objections.

28
00:03:08.713 --> 00:03:10.252
Amara Nwosu: I’m good with it.

29
00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:17.903
Rachel Kim: Great. Diego, can you update the handbook page and note the change? Let’s target two weeks from today.

30
00:03:19.187 --> 00:03:24.956
Diego Santos: Yep, I’ll take care of it. I’ll also add a note about the reporting follow-up.

31
00:03:25.551 --> 00:03:28.628
Rachel Kim: Perfect. I’ll ping Sales Enablement so they’re aware.

32
00:03:29.101 --> 00:03:38.716
Rachel Kim: Okay, last thing—quick heads up: we got the results from the vendor satisfaction survey. Not urgent, but we scored lowest on Marketo support response times.

33
00:03:39.938 --> 00:03:46.861
Diego Santos: Yeah, I saw that. I can draft a ticket for our CSM to discuss at the next check-in.

34
00:03:47.850 --> 00:03:52.465
Nadia Petrova: That’d be great, Diego. It’s been a pain getting timely answers lately.

35
00:03:53.084 --> 00:03:56.545
Omar Haddad: Agreed. Anything we can do to escalate would help.

36
00:03:57.320 --> 00:04:01.551
Rachel Kim: Thanks, Diego. Let’s aim to send that ticket by next Friday.

37
00:04:02.629 --> 00:04:07.244
Diego Santos: Will do. I’ll share the draft in Slack before I send it.

38
00:04:08.109 --> 00:04:10.417
Rachel Kim: Awesome. Anything else before we wrap?

39
00:04:11.648 --> 00:04:13.571
Amara Nwosu: Nope, that’s it from me.

40
00:04:15.019 --> 00:04:16.519
Omar Haddad: All good here.

41
00:04:17.676 --> 00:04:19.214
Nadia Petrova: I’m good, thanks everyone.

42
00:04:20.271 --> 00:04:22.579
Rachel Kim: Alright, have a great weekend, everyone!

43
00:04:23.148 --> 00:04:24.648
Diego Santos: You too!

44
00:04:25.711 --> 00:04:27.211
Amara Nwosu: Bye all!
